Overlay patch 0053: THE HD PACK'S ONE-ROW IMAGES (VR R16 part C item 2).
`vendor/Shipwright/libultraship/src/fast/interpreter.cpp`, LUS at its own pin.

THE SYMPTOM. With `OoT_Reloaded_v11.0.0_4K.o2r` in `Documents/mods`, the
"C-button to Equip" prompt at the bottom of the kaleido item subscreen draws
NOTHING -- in 2D and in VR alike. the user has run that pack since the port
booted; the macOS build has no mods, which is why the oracle never showed it.

THE MEASUREMENT, which is what identified it. `artifacts/vr-r2/device-1.0.1.1.log`
carries overlay 0019's clamp warning 138 times, in exactly two sizes:

    20x   "clamping oversized texture 188416x1"
    118x  "clamping oversized texture 65536x1"

188416 * 4 = 753,664 bytes. The prompt is `gCBtnSymbolsTex`
(`textures/icon_item_static/gCBtnSymbolsTex`, 48x16 IA8, 848 bytes in
`oracle/shiphome/oot.o2r`), loaded at `z_kaleido_scope_PAL.c:2343` by
`gDPLoadTextureBlock(..., icon_w = 46, icon_h = 16, ...)` -- three times per
frame, once per C button, which is where a multiple of 20 per second comes
from. 46 * 16 = 736 bytes requested; a 4K pack replacement of a 48x16 source is
768x256 RGBA, so h_byte_scale = 768*4/48 = 64 and v_pixel_scale = 256/16 = 16,
and 736 * 64 * 16 = 753,664 EXACTLY. That is the arithmetic identification, and
it is the only one that closes: the alternative reading (a 736-wide export
tripping the OOB guard) requires h_byte_scale to be 64 and the image to be 736
wide at the same time, which cannot both be true.

THE MECHANISM IS NOT THE OOB GUARD. `numLoadedBytes` (753,664) is LESS than
`resourceImageSizeBytes` (768*256*4 = 786,432), so the guard at
ImportTextureRaw's "restrict the bytes to a safe amount" never fires here. What
flattens the image is the LOAD BLOCK's stride bookkeeping:
`GfxDpLoadBlock` sets `line_size_bytes = full_image_line_size_bytes =
actual_line_bytes`, and both of its stride refinements require
`texture_to_load.width > 1` -- which the `gDPLoadTextureBlock` macro never
gives (it sets the SetTextureImage width to 1 by construction). So for EVERY
load-block texture the recorded "line" is the WHOLE LOAD, the copy loop runs
once, and the buffer is described as `safeLineSizeBytes/4 x 1`: a
188,416 x 1 strip, which 0019 then clamps to 16384x1 to stop Metal aborting.
A one-row image is a blank icon.

Load-block HD textures normally escape that path through the early return
("Can use the texture directly since it has the correct dimensions"). This one
misses it by ONE TEST, and by a rounding:

    resultOrigLineSize = texture_tile.line_size_bytes = ((46*1)+7)>>3 <<3 = 48
    resultOrigHeight   = 736 / 48 = 15        <-- 15.33 truncated
    resultNewLineSize  = 48 * 64 = 3072 == 4 * 768   OK
    resultNewHeight    = 15 * 16 = 240 != 256        FAILS

The tile stride is 48 bytes and the DMA fetched 736 -- fifteen whole rows and
two thirds of a sixteenth. Vanilla's tile still DESCRIBES sixteen rows (the
rectangle draws all 16), and the pack's replacement has all 256 HD rows. The
row count the comparison wants is the CEILING, not the floor.

THE FIX, two parts, both `__IOS__`-only so the macOS build keeps upstream's
behaviour byte for byte (upstream has this bug too; the oracle runs no pack, so
fixing it there would only remove a comparison point):

  (a) THE EARLY RETURN ALSO ACCEPTS THE CEILING, in a SECOND test placed
      immediately ahead of upstream's rather than by editing it -- so the patch
      removes no upstream line and every case that returned early before still
      does, with the same arguments. For `gCBtnSymbolsTex` it uploads the
      pack's own 768x256 image directly from `addr`, at the stride the tile
      already agrees with (3072 == 4*768). Nothing is copied, nothing is
      clamped, and the icon is the pack author's.

  (b) A BUFFER IS NEVER DESCRIBED AS ONE ROW WHEN ITS STRIDE IS KNOWN. In the
      fallback, when the packed result would be a single row AND the copy was
      contiguous (`safeLineSizeBytes == safeFullImageLineSizeBytes`, the load
      block's signature) AND the resource's own row stride `width*4` divides
      the packed bytes, the upload is redescribed as `width x
      (safeLoadedBytes / (width*4))`. The BYTES ARE NOT TOUCHED -- only their
      shape -- and because the divisibility test proves `width*4` IS the
      stride of the contiguous run, the rows land where they belong (no shear).
      Worst case the image is short a few bottom rows, which is a picture
      instead of a strip. This is what should catch the 118 `65536x1` uploads
      (262,144 bytes each) that this round cannot identify by name.

WHY NOT THE ROW-CLAMP THE BRIEF PROPOSED. Clamping ROWS out of
`resourceImageSizeBytes / safeFullImageLineSizeBytes` while keeping
`safeFullImageLineSizeBytes` as the SOURCE stride is right only when the source
stride and the destination stride differ. Here they are equal and both are the
whole image, so that formula yields rows = 1 and changes nothing; and if it
were fed the pre-guard `fullImageLineSizeBytes` instead, the source rows would
be read at the wrong pitch and the image would shear progressively down the
page. (b) is the same idea done from the side that knows the true stride: the
resource's own width.

WHAT KEEPS US HONEST. 0019's `clamping oversized texture WxH` warning is
DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE and is the regression detector: a device log that still
carries `x1` clamps is a texture family neither (a) nor (b) reached. This patch
is not folded into 0019 for the same reason -- 0019 is the last line of defence
in the Metal backend, this is the description bug one layer up.

--- a/libultraship/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
+++ b/libultraship/src/fast/interpreter.cpp
@@ -1127,6 +1127,27 @@
     uint32_t resultNewLineSize = resultOrigLineSize * metadata->h_byte_scale;
     uint32_t resultNewHeight = resultOrigHeight * metadata->v_pixel_scale;
 
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    // SOH_IOS (overlay 0053): A LOAD BLOCK THAT ENDS MID-ROW STILL DESCRIBES
+    // THAT ROW. gDPLoadTextureBlock(gCBtnSymbolsTex, IA8, 46, 16) fetches
+    // 46*16 = 736 bytes against a tile stride of 48, so resultOrigHeight
+    // truncates 15.33 to 15 and upstream's test below misses by one row --
+    // sending a perfectly good 768x256 HD replacement into the fallback, which
+    // (because a load block records the whole load as a single "line")
+    // describes it as 188416x1 and draws nothing. Accept the CEILING too, in
+    // an ADDED test ahead of upstream's: every case upstream returns early on
+    // still reaches it unchanged, and an exact division makes the two
+    // identical.
+    {
+        uint32_t sohIosRowsCeil =
+            resultOrigLineSize > 0 ? ((numOriginallyLoadedBytes + resultOrigLineSize - 1) / resultOrigLineSize) : 0;
+        uint32_t sohIosNewHeightCeil = (uint32_t)(sohIosRowsCeil * metadata->v_pixel_scale);
+        if (resultNewLineSize == 4 * width && sohIosNewHeightCeil == height) {
+            mRapi->UploadTexture(addr, width, height);
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+#endif
     if (resultNewLineSize == 4 * width && resultNewHeight == height) {
         // Can use the texture directly since it has the correct dimensions
         mRapi->UploadTexture(addr, width, height);
@@ -1162,6 +1183,39 @@
         memset(mTexUploadBuffer + resourceImageSizeBytes, 0, numLoadedBytes - resourceImageSizeBytes);
     }
 
+#ifdef __IOS__
+    // SOH_IOS (overlay 0053): NEVER HAND THE BACKEND A ONE-ROW IMAGE WHEN THE
+    // ROW STRIDE IS KNOWN. A load block records line == full_image_line == the
+    // entire load (GfxDpLoadBlock's two stride refinements both require a
+    // SetTextureImage width > 1, which the gDPLoadTextureBlock macro never
+    // gives), so this fallback always packs "one line" and every HD
+    // replacement that misses the early return above arrives as a 65536x1 or
+    // 188416x1 strip -- which overlay 0019 then clamps to 16384x1 to keep
+    // Metal from aborting. Blank, either way.
+    //
+    // The copy above was CONTIGUOUS whenever the two strides are equal, so the
+    // buffer is a prefix of the replacement image laid out at its own pitch.
+    // If the resource's row stride (width*4) divides the packed bytes, that IS
+    // the pitch: upload the SAME BYTES described as width x rows. No shear is
+    // possible -- the divisibility is what proves the pitch -- and at worst the
+    // image is short its last rows, which is a picture rather than a strip.
+    //
+    // Placed ABOVE the description below, not folded into it, so that overlay
+    // 0020's hunk on those three lines keeps its context intact.
+    {
+        uint32_t sohIosPackedRows = safeLineSizeBytes > 0 ? (safeLoadedBytes / safeLineSizeBytes) : 0;
+        if (sohIosPackedRows <= 1 && width > 0 && safeLineSizeBytes == safeFullImageLineSizeBytes) {
+            uint32_t sohIosStride = (uint32_t)width * 4u;
+            if (safeLineSizeBytes > sohIosStride && (safeLoadedBytes % sohIosStride) == 0) {
+                uint32_t sohIosImageRows = safeLoadedBytes / sohIosStride;
+                if (sohIosImageRows > 1) {
+                    mRapi->UploadTexture(mTexUploadBuffer, (uint32_t)width, sohIosImageRows);
+                    return;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+#endif
     // Describe the buffer by what was actually packed (the loaded HD stride)
     uint32_t uploadWidth = safeLineSizeBytes / 4;
     uint32_t uploadHeight = safeLineSizeBytes > 0 ? safeLoadedBytes / safeLineSizeBytes : 0;
